00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.530
And I know, Haben, you're
here with us at Next.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.920
You just gave a talk on
accessibility in tech

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.540
not too long ago.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.321
Thank you for being
part of the podcast.

00:01:54.321 --> 00:01:55.320
HABEN GIRMA: Absolutely.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.480
It's an honor to be here.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.790
So my name is Haben Girma.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.440
And I am deafblind.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.720
I have limited
vision and hearing.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.070
I can use technology when it's
designed to be accessible.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.710
And it's my hope to
teach more developers

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.230
how to design products and
services that are accessible.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.830
MARK MANDEL: So we have recorded
a full interview with you,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.490
which will be coming up soon.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:19.370
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.120
MARK MANDEL: But to
tease a little of what

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.661
we talked about
previously, could you

00:02:22.661 --> 00:02:24.700
tell us a bit about what
you talked about here

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.824
at Cloud Next?

00:02:25.824 --> 00:02:27.990
HABEN GIRMA: The main
takeaway from the presentation

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.800
this morning is that
developers have the power

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.335
to build services that
everyone can access.

00:02:35.335 --> 00:02:38.950
A lot of people assume that
people with disabilities

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.490
don't use computers or
don't use smartphones,

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:45.160
and ignore the
disability community

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.050
when building services.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.140
Don't make those assumptions.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.770
Design with everyone in mind,

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.760
There are 1.3 billion
people with disabilities

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.180
around the world.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.200
We're the largest
minority group.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.590
People who invest
in accessibility

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.420
get to tap into
this large market.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.300
So it's more business, more
revenue in the long run

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.960
if you design with
access in mind.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.110
So I want to encourage
everyone to stop thinking

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.680
about disability as charity.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.750
Instead, think of it as
a business opportunity.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.050
MELANIE WARRICK: What's
your favorite technology

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.820
that's accessible, and also just
a technology everybody's using?

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.440
HABEN GIRMA: I'm a
huge fan of the iPhone,

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.340
because it has
accessibility built

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.750
in that lots of people
with disabilities

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.917
and non-disabled people can use.

00:03:36.917 --> 00:03:38.500
MELANIE WARRICK: So
mobile phone usage

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.364
in regards to what type of
aspects work best for you?

00:03:42.364 --> 00:03:45.150
HABEN GIRMA: So with my
phone, I now have easy access

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.320
to communicate with people.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:50.630
Before that-- before,
I couldn't text.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.120
And without the ability to
text, if I'm traveling around

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.020
and need to talk to someone,
get environmental information,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.230
maybe an emergency alert, I
wouldn't be able to get that.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.640
But now because I have a
smartphone with accessibility

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.570
built in, I have access
to emergency alerts,

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.820
to fun conversations,
to restaurant reviews.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.660
And I have so much more
freedom now with an accessible

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.660
phone than I did before
accessible phones were

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.784
available.

00:04:22.784 --> 00:04:24.450
MELANIE WARRICK: What
are some resources

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.480
and outside organizations that
you would recommend for people

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.670
to check out if they're
interested in understanding

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.658
how to be better in designing
accessibility in tech?

00:04:32.658 --> 00:04:34.282
HABEN GIRMA: My
website has information

00:04:34.282 --> 00:04:36.050
about accessibility.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:40.020
So you're welcome to check out
my website-- habengirma.com.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.500
Some local organizations--

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.380
San Francisco LightHouse
for the Blind.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:47.810
You can also check out
the National Federation

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:48.800
of the Blind--

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.960
NFE.org or by the National
Association of the Deaf.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.430
There are lots of organizations
that can help provide

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.821
information on accessibility.

00:04:56.821 --> 00:04:58.820
MELANIE WARRICK: What's
been your favorite thing

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.260
at Next so far?

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:02.210
HABEN GIRMA: I love
meeting people.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:05.582
I've had the opportunity to
meet lots of cool people here.

00:05:05.582 --> 00:05:06.540
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.720
Well, Haben, thank you
so much for joining us

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.740
on the podcast today.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.050
And look forward to listening
to the full episode that

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.240
will come out later in
the next few months.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.792
HABEN GIRMA: Thanks
for having me here.

